# Polymers and Biomolecules: The Chemistry of Giants

> The biggest molecules we meet daily — plastics, proteins, DNA,
> starch — are all polymers: small units chained into giants. This
> lesson covers how synthetic plastics are built and classified, then
> tours the four families of molecules that make life possible.

## 1. What Is a Polymer?

[definition]
A **polymer** is a giant molecule built from repeated small units
(**monomers**) joined into a chain, like paper clips linked one after
another. Polyethylene is thousands of ethylene
(CH₂=CH₂) units strong; proteins are chains of amino-acid monomers.
[/definition]

@diagram tree id="polymer-hierarchy" layout="tree" aria="Monomers joining into a polymer chain"
  "Ethylene monomers" -> "CH2=CH2"
  "Ethylene monomers" -> "CH2=CH2"
  "Ethylene monomers" -> "CH2=CH2"
  "CH2=CH2" -> "-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH2- (polyethylene)"
@/diagram

## 2. Making Polymers: Two Routes

- **Addition polymerisation:** monomers with double bonds clip
  together without losing any atoms. Ethylene → polyethylene;
  propylene → polypropylene; vinyl chloride → PVC; tetrafluoroethylene
  → Teflon (PTFE).
- **Condensation polymerisation:** monomers join with the loss of a
  small molecule, usually water. The two growing ends must carry
  complementary groups (e.g. acid + alcohol). Polyesters, polyamides
  (nylon), and every protein are made this way.

## 3. Classifying Plastics by Behaviour

| Class | On heating | Structure | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Thermoplastic | Softens, can be remoulded | Linear/branched chains | PE, PP, PVC, PET, polystyrene |
| Thermosetting | Hardens permanently, chars on reheating | Cross-linked network | Bakelite, melamine, epoxy |
| Elastomer (rubber) | Stretches, snaps back | Coiled, lightly cross-linked | Natural rubber, neoprene, silicone |

[important]
Vulcanisation — Charles Goodyear's 1839 discovery — heats natural
rubber with sulphur, which forges cross-links between chains. More
sulphur means more cross-links: elastic bands (little) to tyre rubber
(moderate) to ebonite (a lot, becoming hard).
[/important]

### Everyday plastics worth knowing

| Plastic | Made from | Uses |
| --- | --- | --- |
| PE (polyethylene) | Ethylene | Bags, bottles, pipes |
| PP (polypropylene) | Propylene | Ropes, food boxes, car parts |
| PVC | Vinyl chloride | Pipes, flooring, insulation |
| PET | Ethylene glycol + terephthalic acid | Drink bottles, fleece |
| PS (polystyrene) | Styrene | Packaging, foam cups |
| Bakelite | Phenol + formaldehyde | Old plugs, switches (first fully synthetic plastic, 1907) |
| Nylon-6,6 | Adipic acid + hexamethylenediamine | Stockings, ropes, gears |

The resin identification codes (1–7) stamped on plastics guide
recycling: PET (1) and HDPE (2) are widely recyclable; the rest,
harder.

## 4. Biomolecules: Life's Four Families

### Carbohydrates — the fuel

Built from carbon, hydrogen, oxygen (~1:2:1 ratio). One unit =
monosaccharide (glucose, fructose); two = disaccharide (sucrose =
glucose + fructose; lactose = glucose + galactose); many joined =
polysaccharide (starch for storage in plants, glycogen in animals,
cellulose for plant cell walls). Humans digest starch but not
cellulose — we lack the enzyme — which is why fibre passes through.

### Proteins — the workers

Chains of **amino acids** (20 kinds), joined by **peptide bonds** — a
condensation polymerisation happening in every cell. The sequence of
amino acids is the **primary structure**; folding into helices and
sheets is secondary; the full 3D shape is tertiary. Shape is
function: enzymes, antibodies, haemoglobin, keratin (hair, nails),
insulin — all proteins.

- Essential amino acids (about 9) cannot be made by the body and must
  come from food.
- Cooking an egg **denatures** its proteins: heat destroys the folded
  shape, which is why the white turns opaque and solid.

### Lipids — the storehouse

Fats and oils are triglycerides: glycerol bonded to three fatty
acids. **Saturated** fatty acids (straight chains, usually animal
fats) stack tightly and are solid at room temperature; **unsaturated**
ones (double bonds kinking the chain, mostly plant oils) stay liquid.
Cholesterol, waxes, and phospholipids (cell membranes) are also
lipids. Fat stores ~37 kJ/g — over twice the energy of carbohydrate
or protein.

### Nucleic acids — the blueprint

**DNA** and **RNA** are polymers of nucleotides. Each nucleotide =
sugar + phosphate + nitrogen base. In DNA, A pairs with T and G with
C across the double helix — hydrogen bonds that make the
hereditary copy both faithful and unzip-able. RNA (single strand,
uracil in place of thymine) carries the recipe from nucleus to
ribosome.

