@/chart

## 5. Synthetic Meets Natural

- **Rayon** (semi-synthetic, 1905): cellulose dissolved and re-spun —
  the first "artificial silk".
- **Polyester and nylon** mimic silk's strength; Kevlar (an aramid)
  is five times stronger than steel by weight — bulletproof vests.
- **Biodegradable polymers** (PLA from corn starch) and the enzyme
  that digests PET (discovered 2016) are sustainability frontiers:
  conventional plastics persist for centuries because few organisms
  can cleave their carbon backbones.

[summary]
Polymers are monomer chains made by addition (no by-product) or
condensation (water out) reactions; thermoplastics can be remoulded,
thermosets cannot, and elastomers snap back thanks to sparse
cross-links. Life runs on four polymer families: carbohydrates
(fuel), proteins (amino-acid workers whose folded shape is function),
lipids (compact energy stores and membranes), and nucleic acids (the
A–T, G–C blueprint of heredity). From Bakelite to Kevlar to
biodegradable PLA, synthetic polymers are condensation and addition
chemistry doing nature's tricks in new ways.
[/summary]
